aiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🟡 Genel Bilgiler (Sarı)#{1}":[30,31],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🟢 Yöntem & Örneklem (Yeşil)":[32,34],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🟢 Yöntem & Örneklem (Yeşil)#{1}":[33,34],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🔴 Bulgular (Kırmızı)":[35,37],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🔴 Bulgular (Kırmızı)#{1}":[36,37],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🔵 Sonuç / Conclusions (Mavi)":[38,40],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🔵 Sonuç / Conclusions (Mavi)#{1}":[39,40],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🟠 Limitasyonlar (Turuncu)":[41,42],"#Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference#🟠 Limitasyonlar (Turuncu)#{1}":[42,42]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Görüntüle","target":"zotero://select/library/items/K66XIVLU","line":22},{"title":"Sayfa 1","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=1&annotation=VRHLDW4L","line":30},{"title":"Sayfa 1","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=1&annotation=5Q3CCSSM","line":33},{"title":"Sayfa 1","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=1&annotation=FP6B96BQ","line":33},{"title":"Sayfa 7","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=7&annotation=VSRDECN2","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 7","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=7&annotation=XSXPEZRS","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 8","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=8&annotation=EU5JJUHS","line":36},{"title":"Sayfa 8","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=8&annotation=XZDWDZJS","line":39},{"title":"Sayfa 8","target":"zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3WBDRYV5?page=8&annotation=VFUSANL2","line":42}],"metadata":{"Title":"Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals: A Focus on Rice and Congee, With Milk as a Low-Glycemic Reference","Authors":"Hsien-Yu Fan, Pey-Rong Chen, Ya-Ting Kuo, Yu-Tien Chang, Kuo-Liong Chien, Yung-Chi Lu, Shih-Lin Hung, Shih-Ming Chuang, Ying-Yueh Chu","Year":2025,"DOI":"10.1016/j.cdnut.2025.107569","Nitelik":null,"Alan":null,"Konu":null,"AI_Methodology":null,"Layer_Focus":null,"Model_Asamasi":null,"Proposed_Tool":null,"Methods":"this study aims to enhance portion size estimation methods, with a particular focus on rice and congee, using milk as a low-glycemic index reference in selected Taiwanese hospitals.We used a RealSense D435i depth camera to capture both 2-dimensional (2D) and 3-dimensional (3D) images of rice, congee, and milk served in standard hospital bowls. The images were labeled and classified by a convolutional neural network, Xception, whereas food weight and volume were estimated using 25 depth points with multiple perceptron (MLP) models. MLP models were trained on 80% of the data set and tested on the remaining 20%, with predictions refined through a weighted ensemble of selected MLP models. We validated the MLP model performance through 3 comparisons: 1) MLP with traditional statistical methods (linear regression), 2) MLP with clinical dietitians, and 3) MLP performance across 3 different hospital settings.","Findings":"findings The Xception model demonstrated strong performance in classifying foods with high glycemic index and milk as a low- glycemic reference, showing steadily increasing accuracy and decreasing loss during training, suggesting effective learning and minimal overfitting. In our quantitative analysis of hospital leftover foods, MLP models accurately predicted the weights of rice and congee, as well as the volume of fresh milkfood types, the models consistently outperfotional linear regression, showing lower RMSE and values.Furthermore, when compared with estimates from 6 experi­ enced dietitians, the MLP models again showed superior accu­ racy. Notably, the model’s performance remained consistent across 3 different hospital sites, indicating strong generalizability.ocket expenses, all pa­ This study offers a potential solution to the shortage of dietitian resources in Taiwanese hospitals. By using CNN and MLP models to identify and quan­ tify the remaining grams of high-GI foods when meals are returned, our MLP model has achieved a margin of error of <3% in gram estimationelectronic medical","Conclusions":"These results demonstrate the robustness of the combined Xception and MLP approach in food quantification, with potential for further refinement in predicting lower quan­ tities and extending the method to additional food type","Limitations":"The data set used, although carefully curated, may not fully represent the diversity of hospital food items, which could limit the generalizability of the findings to other settings or types of meals. Furthermore, the study’s focus on specific food types like rice, congee, and milk means that the models may require further validation and potentially retraining to handle a broader range of foods with varying textures and appearances. Another limitation is the reliance on a single imaging device, which, although standardized in this study, may not capture the same level of detail in different environments or under varying lighting con­ ditions. Finally, the study’s findings are based on controlled experimental conditions, and additional research is needed to assess the models’ performance in more dynamic, real-world hospital environments where factors such as varying plate sizes, different food consistencies, and patient handling of food could impact accuracyundergo","User_Notes":""},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[],"last_embed":{"hash":"cs9faz","at":1769791476231}},"smart_blocks:Applying a Robust Deep Learning Model to Assess the Remaining Amount of Foods With High Glycemic Index in Taiwanese Hospital Diabetic Meals.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"embeddings":{"TaylorAI/bge-micro-v2":{"vec":[-0.02388423,-0.01235207,0.02493141,0.00557652,0.04789016,0.05065601,-0.03231544,0.04093742,0.00275483,-0.04303193,0.00323076,-0.08419943,0.02141525,0.05505973,0.02798742,-0.0462654,0.00871177,-0.00782317,-0.07427631,-0.02647823,0.06598726,-0.05258143,0.00214645,-0.03507501,0.11075309,-0.028086,0.02873477,-0.01251254,-0.08421133,-0.26650369,0.03210934,0.05628608,0.07800848,-0.03708138,-0.04533634,0.00279247,-0.02023583,0.02042081,-0.00203363,0.00565917,0.0679543,0.00162118,0.0302397,-0.01250983,0.03771989,-0.07707946,-0.05621687,-0.00882193,0.00343788,-0.03666722,-0.01854755,-0.01770599,0.0189428,0.0496239,0.05181694,0.02980296,0.06690855,-0.0119683,0.0162484,0.03479458,0.0571087,0.07596347,-0.18719047,0.03331062,0.03273591,0.0138919